+#include <cstdint>
+#include <optional>
+
+// A UI process that loaded an older WebKit speaks the message enumeration that
+// release shipped, which is not the one this tree compiles: `MessageName` is a
+// dense enumeration over every message in the tree: this one has 4086 entries and
+// the shipped 16.1 enumeration has 3185, so 4086 - 3185 = 901 entries were
+// inserted, and any one of them landing before a name reassigns that name and
+// every number after it. Almost every number moves even where the name survives.
+// The two functions below convert
+// between the two numberings; the tables they switch over are generated by
+// scripts/gen-ipc-message-number-tables.py.
+//
+// This gate decides only whether the tables are compiled in. It does not decide
+// whether a connection uses them: that is IPC::Connection::m_shouldTranslateMessageNames,
+// a member that stays at its `{ false }` initialiser. No code in this tree calls
+// setShouldTranslateMessageNames, so on every connection the flag is false and
+// the tables below are compiled but unreachable.
+//
+// The gate and the flag answer different questions and only the second may be
+// acted on. The gate asks what this binary was built to support. The flag asks
+// what the peer across a live connection actually speaks.
+//
+// The deployment target cannot stand in for the peer question. Measured by
+// numbering every message in this tree and in an older enumeration and comparing
+// index by index, with scripts/gen-ipc-message-number-tables.py as the extractor.
+// This tree has 4086 numbers and the shipped 16.1 enumeration
+// (releases/Apple/Safari-16.1-iOS-16.1) has 3185, so no 16.1 number falls off
+// this enumeration's end: the mismatch is not one of size. Of the 2911 names the
+// two enumerations share, 5 happen to carry the same number on both sides and
+// 2906 do not. 274 names exist only in 16.1 and 1175 only in this tree.
+//
+// That misalignment is what makes the handshake fail, and `InitializeConnection`
+// is the case to read. `InitializeConnection` is number 3076 in this tree. The
+// shipped 16.1 enumeration puts it at 2478, and 2478 is the entry the incoming
+// table needs for it: `case 2478: return MessageName::InitializeConnection;`.
+// Against the shipped 16.1 enumeration the number at this build's 3076 is
+// MessageName::RemoteMediaPlayerProxy_VideoFrameForCurrentTimeIfChanged, which is
+// what an incoming 16.1 number 3076 would become, and it is not the handshake.
+// 3076 is not in isKnownUnmappedIncomingNumber, so the miss path that would flag
+// the message is not taken and the substitution is silent.
+// ConnectionCocoa.mm's `decoder->messageName() == MessageName::InitializeConnection`
+// then never fires and the connection never completes its handshake. Setting the
+// flag for a peer that already numbers messages this way does not degrade a
+// connection, it breaks the handshake on the first message.
+//
+// The tables this file carries are generated from that shipped 16.1 tag, so this
+// is the substitution they would make, not a hypothetical one: the incoming table
+// reads a 16.1 number and the outgoing table writes one, and both were built from
+// releases/Apple/Safari-16.1-iOS-16.1's own generate-message-receiver.py output.
+// A 16.1 number the tables leave unlisted is one this build has no receiver for,
+// and isKnownUnmappedIncomingNumber names every such number it knows about.
+//
+// Two deployment routes exist and they differ only in which WebKit.framework the
+// UI process loads at run time, never in how this binary was compiled:
+//
+//   route A, mixed generations: the UI process keeps the system framework on the
+//     device, which is releases/Apple/Safari-16.1-iOS-16.1, and only the
+//     auxiliary processes load this build, so the peer numbers its messages with
+//     that enumeration and a connection would have to translate. A route-A build
+//     is the only one that may set the flag, and it needs tables generated from
+//     that tag, which is what this file's tables already are.
+//   route B, same generation: scripts/install-webkit-override.sh puts this
+//     build's WebKit.framework on the UI process's DYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH, so both
+//     sides run this build and number messages identically. The flag must be
+//     false.
+//
+// This artefact is route B, and the reason it is decided here rather than
+// detected is that no build setting can tell the two apart. The workflow passes
+// IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET 16.1 either way (build-webkit.yml:29,
+// WK_BUILD_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET), which makes this gate 1 in both routes: it says
+// this binary can talk to a 16.1 peer, not that it will. No run-time signal can
+// decide it either. "client-sdk-version" is written by no iOS tag and the code
+// that read it is deleted. The "client-sdk-aligned-behaviors" bitmap does arrive
+// before AuxiliaryProcess::initialize runs - XPCServiceEntryPoint.h calls
+// getClientSDKAlignedBehaviors and then initializeAuxiliaryProcess - and its
+// width does separate 16.0-16.3 (8 bytes) from 16.4 and later (12 bytes), but
+// 16.4 through 17.x are also 12 bytes, the same as this build, so width does not
+// name a generation; and getClientSDKAlignedBehaviors rejects a foreign payload
+// without keeping its value, so nothing reaches the caller.
+//
+// A route-A build is where these tables start being used, and they are the tables
+// such a build needs: the older side of both is generated from
+// releases/Apple/Safari-16.1-iOS-16.1 (614.2.9.0.10), the same build the system
+// framework on the device is. Its anchors are firstSynchronousOldMessageNumber
+// 2946, InitializeConnection 2478 and Last 3184, all three read out of the header
+// that tag's own generate-message-receiver.py wrote. Those numbers are the ones
+// the tables must carry, and the checks in scripts/check-translation-switch.py
+// pin them so a table built on another tag fails the build.
+//
+// scripts/regen-ipc-message-number-headers.py reproduces both headers from the
+// two tags, and scripts/gen-ipc-message-number-tables.py builds the tables from
+// what it writes. Regenerating from another tag is a supported operation; it is
+// also the only way to change these numbers, because the generator has no default
+// input path and script-checked anchors would otherwise still name the old ones.
+//
+// scripts/check-translation-switch.py enforces the part a compiler cannot see:
+// that no call to setShouldTranslateMessageNames exists in the shipped tree, and
+// that this file still records why. The workflow runs it after the patch is
+// applied, so a re-added call fails the build instead of waiting for someone to
+// run the script by hand. Regenerating the tables for a real route A has to
+// update that script too.
+#if PLATFORM(IOS) && defined(__IPHONE_OS_VERSION_MIN_REQUIRED) && __IPHONE_OS_VERSION_MIN_REQUIRED < 180000
+#define IOS_16_MESSAGE_NUMBER_TRANSLATION 1
+#else
+#define IOS_16_MESSAGE_NUMBER_TRANSLATION 0
+#endif
+
+// The tables are for the route that can meet an older peer, and this build is
+// not that route: they are compiled in so the generated code keeps being checked
+// against the tree, and left unreachable because nothing sets the flag.
+#if IOS_16_MESSAGE_NUMBER_TRANSLATION
+
+namespace IPC {
+
+// Converts a message number this build assigned into the number the iOS 16
+// enumeration assigns to the same message, for a message about to be sent to an
+// iOS 16 UI process. Returns MessageName::Invalid when this build has a message
+// the older enumeration does not name; the caller checks
+// isKnownUnmappedOutgoingName first and skips the send.
+MessageName translateOutgoingMessageName(MessageName);
+
+// Converts a message number received from an iOS 16 UI process into the number
+// this build assigns to the same message. Returns MessageName::Invalid when the
+// older enumeration names something this build does not; the caller checks
+// isKnownUnmappedIncomingNumber first, because Invalid is synchronous and would
+// otherwise take dispatchSyncMessage down a path that returns without replying.
+MessageName translateIncomingMessageName(MessageName);
+
+// True for a message this build has and the older enumeration does not. Every
+// such case carries the guard that keeps its enumerator present, so this
+// compiles in exactly the configurations that have the message.
+bool isKnownUnmappedOutgoingName(MessageName);
+
+// The incoming counterpart. Takes the number rather than a MessageName because
+// an unmapped number has no name in this build's enumeration to pass.
+bool isKnownUnmappedIncomingNumber(uint16_t);
+
+// The first synchronous message number in the older enumeration, defined beside
+// the tables that read it. A caller uses it to tell, among the messages it
+// drops, the ones whose sender is waiting for a reply from the ones whose sender
+// is not.
+extern const uint16_t firstSynchronousOldMessageNumber;
+
+// One message this build cannot map is worth naming, because dropping it looks
+// alarming and is not. In the shipped 16.1 enumeration `Terminate` is number
+// 2483, and 2483 is in this file's isKnownUnmappedIncomingNumber list. 16.1 does
+// send it: NetworkProcessProxy::didBecomeUnresponsive
+// (UIProcess/Network/NetworkProcessProxy.cpp:172) constructs it once the network
+// process has gone unresponsive for the third time inside 15 seconds, and 16.1's
+// Connection::processIncomingMessage answers it by calling
+// terminateDueToIPCTerminateMessage (Platform/IPC/Connection.cpp:788), which
+// CRASHes. This build has neither: 18.3 deleted both the enumerator and that
+// sender.
+//
+// Dropping the message is safe, and the number is why it needs no reply path.
+// 2483 is below firstSynchronousOldMessageNumber 2946, so the message is
+// asynchronous and the sender is not holding a reply slot open for it. The 16.1
+// sender also arms a one-second timer that calls terminate() and invalidates the
+// connection, so the auxiliary process goes away on the sender's schedule
+// regardless of what this build does with the message.
+
+} // namespace IPC
+
+#endif // IOS_16_MESSAGE_NUMBER_TRANSLATION
