+
+    // Nothing here calls setShouldTranslateMessageNames, so every Connection keeps the `{ false }`
+    // its member initialises with.
+    //
+    // The tables are still compiled in: IOS_16_MESSAGE_NUMBER_TRANSLATION keys on the deployment
+    // target, which is 16.1. That macro describes how this framework was built, not what is on the
+    // other end of the connection. Under this build's deployment the UI process loads this same
+    // framework through the override, so both sides run this same enumeration and number a message
+    // identically.
+    //
+    // Turning it on would not be a no-op. The two enumerations share 2911 names, and 2906 of them
+    // sit at a different position, so an incoming number is looked up as a different message. The
+    // handshake goes first: this build's InitializeConnection is at position 3076 of the generated
+    // enumeration, and the table's case for 3076 returns
+    // RemoteMediaPlayerProxy_VideoFrameForCurrentTimeIfChanged,
+    // so the `messageName() == MessageName::InitializeConnection` test in ConnectionCocoa.mm stops
+    // matching and the connection never establishes.
+    //
+    // This is also why the setter may not be turned on merely because the deployment target is 16.1:
+    // the target is a property of the build, while what decides whether two peers agree is which
+    // enumeration each of them runs. A build genuinely paired with an older UI process is a
+    // different case, and there the call belongs: once, on this connection.
     initializeConnection(m_connection.get());
     m_connection->open(*this);
 }
